THOUGHT: NullAway complains because `value` is nullable but `returnType()` dereferences it unconditionally. The logic already has `returnsNull()` and uses `returnsNull()` in the caller, so `returnType()` should never be called when `value` is null. To satisfy NullAway without changing behavior, I’ll add an explicit null check and throw an IllegalStateException if `value` is null. Then I’ll run the build to confirm the error count drops by one and no new errors appear.

```bash
cd /home/vscode/nullness-benchmarks/mockito && sed -i '54,56c\  private Class<?> returnType() {
    if (value == null) {
      throw new IllegalStateException("Cannot determine return type when value is null");
    }
    return value.getClass();
  }' src/main/java/org/mockito/internal/stubbing/answers/Returns.java && ./gradlew compileJava -x test -q > new_build_output.log 2>&1 && grep -i "NullAway" -n new_build_output.log | tail -n 5
```

